<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for citeck-launcher.app.

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime (the codesign "options runtime"
  flag), which by default forbids the JIT and the RWX mappings JavaScriptCore
  needs. The Wails webview embeds WebKit, so without these the app notarizes
  fine but crashes the moment it renders the UI.

  Keep this list minimal: every entitlement here is attack surface, and
  Apple's notary service flags gratuitous ones.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- JavaScriptCore JIT inside the embedded WebKit view. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- WebKit maps unsigned executable pages for JIT-compiled JS. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
<!--
  NOT granted, deliberately:

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — an earlier draft included it
  "so dyld can load the system frameworks CGO links against". That reasoning is
  wrong: Apple's own platform frameworks are exempt from library validation, and
  the daemon child process ships inside this bundle under the same signing
  identity. Granting it would be real attack surface for no benefit. Add it back
  only if a signed build actually fails to load a third-party dylib.

  These entitlements only take effect on the Developer ID path, which applies the
  hardened runtime; the ad-hoc fallback used today passes neither. So the first
  real signed build is the first time they are exercised — if the UI renders and
  the daemon starts, they are right. If WKWebView turns out not to need the two
  JIT entitlements either (it hosts JS in a separate system process), drop them
  too rather than carrying them by superstition.
-->

</plist>
